Wait — but callbacks should fire "when the cell's value in the new stable state differs from the previous stable state." Since we recompute in topological order, each cell is recomputed exactly once per propagation (Kahn guarantees each affected cell is recomputed exactly once), and by the time it's recomputed, all of its affected inputs have their final values. So the comparison `self._value != old_value` is exactly the comparison "new stable state vs. previous stable state."

One caveat: the callback receives the new value. The test's callback signature: `callback(value)` (partial with observer). Good.

Also, should the callback fire on the initial computation? No — the initial value is the first stable state, and there's no "previous stable state" to differ from. In `__init__`, we set the value without firing the callback (and there are no callbacks yet at that point anyway).

